    "PAPER - I\n\nFoundations of Psychology\n\n1. Introduction: Definition of Psychology; Historical antecedents of Psychology and trends in the 21st century; Psychology and scientific methods; Psychology in relation to other social sciences and natural sciences; Application of Psychology to societal problems.\n\n2. Methods of Psychology: Types of research: Descriptive, evaluative, diagnostic and prognostic; Methods of research: survey, observation, case-study and experiments; Characteristics of experimental and non-experimental designs; Quasi-experimental designs; Focused group discussions, brainstorming, grounded theory approach.\n\n3. Research Methods: Major steps in psychological research (problem statement, hypothesis formulation, research designs, sampling, tools of data collection, analysis and interpretation, report writing); Fundamental versus applied research; Methods of data collection (interview, observation, questionnaire); Research designs (ex-post facto and experimental); Application of statistical techniques (t-test, two-way ANOVA, correlation, regression, factor analysis); Item Response Theory.\n\n4. Development of Human Behaviour: Growth and development; Principles of development; Role of genetic and environmental factors in determining human behaviour; Influence of cultural factors in socialization; Life span development—characteristics, development tasks, promoting psychological well-being across stages of life span.\n\n5. Sensation, Attention and Perception: Sensation—thresholds, signal detection, vigilance; Attention—factors including set and stimulus characteristics; Perception—definition and concept, biological factors, perceptual organization, influence of experience, perceptual defence, space and depth perception, size estimation, perceptual readiness; Plasticity of perception; Extrasensory perception; Culture and perception; Subliminal perception.\n\n6. Learning: Concepts and theories (Behaviourist, Gestalt, Information Processing); Extinction, discrimination, generalization; Programmed learning, probability learning, self-instructional learning; Reinforcement schedules; Escape, avoidance, punishment; Modeling and social learning.\n\n7. Memory: Encoding and remembering; Short-term, long-term, sensory memory (iconic, echoic); Multistore model; Levels of processing; Mnemonics; Theories of forgetting (decay, interference, retrieval failure); Metamemory; Amnesia (anterograde, retrograde).\n\n8. Thinking and Problem Solving: Piaget’s theory; Concept formation; Information processing; Reasoning and problem solving; Facilitators and barriers; Creative thinking and creativity; Decision making and judgment; Recent trends.\n\n9. Motivation and Emotion: Psychological and physiological basis; Measurement; Effects on behaviour; Intrinsic vs extrinsic motivation; Emotional competence and related issues.\n\n10. Intelligence and Aptitude: Concepts and theories (Spearman, Thurstone, Guilford, Vernon, Sternberg, J.P. Das); Emotional and social intelligence; Measurement; IQ and deviation IQ; Fluid and crystallized intelligence; Multiple intelligence.\n\n11. Personality: Definition and theories (psychoanalytic, behaviouristic, humanistic, trait, type, etc.); Measurement (projective and objective tests); Indian approach; Big Five theory; Self-concept.\n\n12. Attitudes, Values and Interests: Components and formation; Measurement; Attitude change; Stereotypes and prejudice; Attribution theories; Recent trends.\n\n13. Language and Communication: Properties and structure of language; Language acquisition theories (Skinner, Chomsky); Communication types; Effective communication training.\n\n14. Issues and Perspectives in Modern Psychology: Computer applications; Artificial intelligence; Psychocybernetics; Consciousness; Sleep, dreams, meditation; Hypnosis; Drug-induced states; Extrasensory perception; Intersensory perception.\n\nPAPER – II\n\nPsychology: Issues and Applications\n\n1. Psychological Measurement of Individual Differences: Nature of individual differences; Standardized tests; Types, use, misuse, limitations; Ethical issues.\n\n2. Psychological Well-being and Mental Disorders: Health and illness; Positive health; Anxiety, mood, schizophrenia, personality and substance disorders; Life style and well-being; Happiness.\n\n3. Therapeutic Approaches: Psychodynamic, behavioural, client-centred, cognitive therapies; Indigenous approaches (yoga, meditation); Biofeedback; Rehabilitation and mental health promotion.\n\n4. Work Psychology and Organizational Behaviour: Selection and training; HR development; Motivation theories (Maslow, Herzberg, Vroom, etc.); Leadership; Stress management; Ergonomics; Consumer psychology; Organizational behaviour.\n\n5. Educational Psychology Applications: Teaching-learning process; Learning styles; Gifted, slow learners, learning disabilities; Memory training; Guidance and counselling; Tests in education.\n\n6. Community Psychology: Small groups; Social action; Community awareness; Group decision making; Social change.\n\n7. Rehabilitation Psychology: Prevention levels; Rehabilitation of physically, mentally, socially challenged; Substance abuse, delinquency, HIV/AIDS victims; Role of social agencies.\n\n8. Psychology and Disadvantaged Groups: Deprivation; Social, cultural, economic impacts; Education and motivation; Relative deprivation.\n\n9. Social Integration Issues: Caste, class, religion, language conflicts; Prejudice; In-group/out-group behaviour; Conflict resolution; Social integration.\n\n10. Psychology in IT and Mass Media: Role of psychologists; Training; Distance learning; E-commerce; Media influence; Value formation; IT consequences.\n\n11. Psychology and Economic Development: Achievement motivation; Entrepreneurship; Training; Consumer awareness; Government policies.\n\n12. Environment and Psychology: Noise, pollution, crowding; Population psychology; Environmental degradation; Motivation for sustainable behaviour.\n\n13. Applied Psychology Fields: Military, sports, media influence, terrorism psychology; Testing, training, counselling; Performance enhancement.\n\n14. Psychology of Gender: Discrimination; Diversity management; Glass ceiling; Self-fulfilling prophecy; Women and society."
